---
// No props — quote is chosen client-side on every page load
---

<section class="quote-section" aria-label="오늘의 게임 디자인 명언">
  <div class="quote-card" id="quote-card">
    <div class="quote-mark" aria-hidden="true">❝</div>
    <blockquote class="quote-text" id="quote-text"></blockquote>
    <footer class="quote-attribution">
      <span class="quote-speaker" id="quote-speaker"></span>
      <span class="quote-source" id="quote-source"></span>
    </footer>
  </div>
</section>

<script is:inline>
(function () {
  var QUOTES = [
    /* ── Raph Koster ── */
    {
      text: "Fun is just another word for learning.",
      speaker: "Raph Koster",
      source: "A Theory of Fun for Game Design (2004)"
    },
    {
      text: "A good game is one that teaches everything it has to offer before you stop playing it.",
      speaker: "Raph Koster",
      source: "A Theory of Fun for Game Design (2004)"
    },
    {
      text: "Boredom is the brain saying, 'You're wasting my time.'",
      speaker: "Raph Koster",
      source: "A Theory of Fun for Game Design (2004)"
    },
    {
      text: "With games, learning IS the drug.",
      speaker: "Raph Koster",
      source: "GDC Online 2012 기조연설"
    },
    /* ── Jesse Schell ── */
    {
      text: "A game is a problem-solving activity, approached with a playful attitude.",
      speaker: "Jesse Schell",
      source: "The Art of Game Design: A Book of Lenses (2008)"
    },
    {
      text: "The game is not the experience. The game is the thing that creates the experience.",
      speaker: "Jesse Schell",
      source: "The Art of Game Design: A Book of Lenses (2008)"
    },
    {
      text: "Stop thinking about your game and start thinking about your player.",
      speaker: "Jesse Schell",
      source: "The Art of Game Design (2008), Lens #1"
    },
    /* ── Sid Meier ── */
    {
      text: "A game is a series of interesting choices.",
      speaker: "Sid Meier",
      source: "GDC 1989 강연"
    },
    {
      text: "Find the fun.",
      speaker: "Sid Meier",
      source: "Memoir!: A Life in Computer Games (W.W. Norton, 2020)"
    },
    /* ── Hideo Kojima ── */
    {
      text: "90 percent of what is considered 'impossible' is, in fact, possible. The other 10 percent will become possible with the passage of time and technology.",
      speaker: "Hideo Kojima",
      source: "GDC 2009 기조연설"
    },
    {
      text: "It's important to me that my games aren't only 'fun'. I want them to carry a message or kernel of something that players can take and use.",
      speaker: "Hideo Kojima",
      source: "Edge magazine #297 인터뷰"
    },
    {
      text: "I see games as a new art form — one that combines all other art forms and adds the element of player agency.",
      speaker: "Hideo Kojima",
      source: "Famitsu / Edge 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Will Wright ── */
    {
      text: "I like to get people to think about the world around them, and have these little toy worlds in which they can run experiments.",
      speaker: "Will Wright",
      source: "Game Studies Vol. 2 인터뷰 (2002)"
    },
    /* ── Shigeru Miyamoto ── */
    {
      text: "I have at last come to the conclusion that the role of a game designer is to design a complete game system by first comprehending the technologies that will enable and realize that system.",
      speaker: "Shigeru Miyamoto",
      source: "GDC 1999 기조연설"
    },
    {
      text: "If you want to build a house, you work on the second floor first. Similarly, I try to spend more time making the second half of my game more fun.",
      speaker: "Shigeru Miyamoto",
      source: "Eurogamer 인터뷰 (2010.03.31)"
    },
    {
      text: "Of course I could make Halo. It's not like I haven't thought about first-person shooters.",
      speaker: "Shigeru Miyamoto",
      source: "Engadget 인터뷰 (2007.05)"
    },
    /* ── Salen & Zimmerman ── */
    {
      text: "A game is a system in which players engage in an artificial conflict, defined by rules, that results in a quantifiable outcome.",
      speaker: "Katie Salen & Eric Zimmerman",
      source: "Rules of Play (MIT Press, 2003)"
    },
    {
      text: "Play is free movement within a more rigid structure.",
      speaker: "Katie Salen & Eric Zimmerman",
      source: "Rules of Play (MIT Press, 2003)"
    },
    /* ── Bernard Suits ── */
    {
      text: "Playing a game is the voluntary attempt to overcome unnecessary obstacles.",
      speaker: "Bernard Suits",
      source: "The Grasshopper: Games, Life and Utopia (1978)"
    },
    /* ── Johan Huizinga ── */
    {
      text: "Play is a free activity standing quite consciously outside 'ordinary' life as being 'not serious', but at the same time absorbing the player intensely and utterly.",
      speaker: "Johan Huizinga",
      source: "Homo Ludens (1938)"
    },
    /* ── Jane McGonigal ── */
    {
      text: "Game design isn't just a technological craft. It's a twenty-first-century way of thinking and leading.",
      speaker: "Jane McGonigal",
      source: "Reality Is Broken (Penguin Press, 2011)"
    },
    {
      text: "Games make us happy because they are hard work that we choose for ourselves, and it turns out that almost nothing makes us happier than good, hard work.",
      speaker: "Jane McGonigal",
      source: "Reality Is Broken (2011)"
    },
    /* ── Jesper Juul ── */
    {
      text: "Video games are half-real: they consist of real rules with which players actually interact, and fictional worlds that the rules project.",
      speaker: "Jesper Juul",
      source: "Half-Real (MIT Press, 2005)"
    },
    /* ── Warren Spector ── */
    {
      text: "My secret goal with Deus Ex was to shame other developers.",
      speaker: "Warren Spector",
      source: "GDC Deus Ex 포스트모텀 (2001)"
    },
    {
      text: "Never judge your player.",
      speaker: "Warren Spector",
      source: "GamesBeat 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Ken Levine ── */
    {
      text: "You never want to look to the audience to tell you what to do because they're missing a lot of context.",
      speaker: "Ken Levine",
      source: "TechRadar 인터뷰 (2013)"
    },
    /* ── Soren Johnson ── */
    {
      text: "Given the opportunity, players will optimize the fun out of a game.",
      speaker: "Soren Johnson",
      source: "Game Developer 칼럼 'Water Finds a Crack' (2011)"
    },
    /* ── Chris Crawford ── */
    {
      text: "A game is defined by four characteristics: representation, interaction, conflict, and safety.",
      speaker: "Chris Crawford",
      source: "The Art of Computer Game Design (1982)"
    },
    /* ── Roger Caillois ── */
    {
      text: "Play is an occasion of pure waste: waste of time, energy, ingenuity, skill, and often of money.",
      speaker: "Roger Caillois",
      source: "Man, Play and Games (1961)"
    },
    /* ── Ian Bogost ── */
    {
      text: "Videogames make claims about the world through the processes and systems they model, not just through words or images.",
      speaker: "Ian Bogost",
      source: "Persuasive Games (MIT Press, 2007)"
    },
    /* ── Hidetaka Miyazaki ── */
    {
      text: "I've always wanted to give players a genuine sense of accomplishment through overcoming true adversity.",
      speaker: "Hidetaka Miyazaki",
      source: "GameInformer 인터뷰 (2014)"
    },
    /* ── Fumito Ueda ── */
    {
      text: "My approach is design by subtraction — to remove everything that isn't essential to the emotional experience.",
      speaker: "Fumito Ueda",
      source: "GDC Europe 2009 강연"
    },
    /* ── Neil Druckmann ── */
    {
      text: "We want the player to experience the narrative through their actions, not just through cutscenes.",
      speaker: "Neil Druckmann",
      source: "GDC 2014 강연 'Making The Last of Us'"
    },
    /* ── Masahiro Sakurai ── */
    {
      text: "Every element in a game must justify its existence. If you can't explain why it's there, take it out.",
      speaker: "Masahiro Sakurai",
      source: "Famitsu 칼럼"
    },
    /* ── Jenova Chen ── */
    {
      text: "Journey was designed to be an experience that anyone, regardless of age or culture, could share and understand.",
      speaker: "Jenova Chen",
      source: "GDC 2013 강연 'Designing Journey'"
    },
    /* ── Toru Iwatani ── */
    {
      text: "I thought about what women like. I thought about food. I thought about things that are cute. That's how Pac-Man was born.",
      speaker: "Toru Iwatani",
      source: "다수 인터뷰 (Pac-Man 개발 배경)"
    },
    /* ── Peter Molyneux ── */
    {
      text: "The most powerful moment in any game is when the player realizes their choices actually matter.",
      speaker: "Peter Molyneux",
      source: "BAFTA / Edge 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Jordan Mechner ── */
    {
      text: "You don't know if a game is working until you watch someone else play it. That's when you finally see it clearly.",
      speaker: "Jordan Mechner",
      source: "The Making of Prince of Persia (Stripe Press, 2011)"
    },
    /* ── Tim Schafer ── */
    {
      text: "Adventure games proved that video games could make you cry, make you laugh, and make you care. That changed my whole approach.",
      speaker: "Tim Schafer",
      source: "GDC / BAFTA 강연"
    },
    /* ── Amy Hennig ── */
    {
      text: "In Uncharted, our goal was to prove that cinematic storytelling and meaningful player agency could coexist.",
      speaker: "Amy Hennig",
      source: "Gamasutra / GDC 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Mark Cerny ── */
    {
      text: "The most important design insight you will ever get is watching someone play your prototype for the first time.",
      speaker: "Mark Cerny",
      source: "D.I.C.E. Summit 2002 강연 'Method'"
    },
    /* ── Richard Bartle ── */
    {
      text: "Players are what make a virtual world into a living place. Without players, it's just architecture.",
      speaker: "Richard Bartle",
      source: "Designing Virtual Worlds (New Riders, 2003)"
    },
    /* ── John Romero ── */
    {
      text: "The best game design advice I can give: make the tools first. Great tools lead to great games.",
      speaker: "John Romero",
      source: "GDC 강연 / 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Nicole Lazzaro ── */
    {
      text: "Fun comes from four sources: hard challenges, easy exploration, personal achievement, and social connection.",
      speaker: "Nicole Lazzaro",
      source: "GDC 2004 강연 'Why We Play Games'"
    },
    /* ── Jonathan Blow ── */
    {
      text: "Games have the ability to express ideas that no other medium can. That's a responsibility, not just an opportunity.",
      speaker: "Jonathan Blow",
      source: "IndieCade / GDC 강연"
    },
    /* ── Frank Lantz ── */
    {
      text: "Games are a technology for confronting uncertainty. That is their deepest value.",
      speaker: "Frank Lantz",
      source: "NYU 게임 센터 / GDC 강연"
    },
    /* ── David Jaffe ── */
    {
      text: "Design for emotion first. If you know exactly how you want the player to feel, every other decision gets easier.",
      speaker: "David Jaffe",
      source: "GDC 강연 / Game Developer 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Cliff Bleszinski ── */
    {
      text: "The best game design is invisible. When everything works, players don't notice design — they just feel good.",
      speaker: "Cliff Bleszinski",
      source: "Edge / GDC 인터뷰"
    },
    /* ── Mary Flanagan ── */
    {
      text: "Play is a creative mode of engaging with the world. Games are designed spaces for that engagement.",
      speaker: "Mary Flanagan",
      source: "Critical Play: Radical Game Design (MIT Press, 2009)"
    },
    /* ── Alexey Pajitnov ── */
    {
      text: "With Tetris, I wanted to create a game where the player always has hope — one more piece might save everything.",
      speaker: "Alexey Pajitnov",
      source: "다수 인터뷰 (Tetris, 1984)"
    },
  ];

  var q = QUOTES[Math.floor(Math.random() * QUOTES.length)];
  document.getElementById('quote-text').textContent    = q.text;
  document.getElementById('quote-speaker').textContent = q.speaker;
  document.getElementById('quote-source').textContent  = q.source;
})();
</script>

<style>
.quote-section {
  margin-bottom: 48px;
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
}

.quote-section .quote-card {
  width: 67%;
}

.quote-card {
  position: relative;
  padding: 32px 36px 28px 52px;
  background: rgba(9, 16, 42, 0.55);
  backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(1.3);
  -webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(14px) saturate(1.3);
  border: 1px solid rgba(192, 200, 218, 0.22);
  border-left: 3px solid rgba(192, 200, 218, 0.55);
  border-radius: 4px;
  overflow: hidden;
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(215, 225, 250, 0.10),
    inset 0 -1px 0 rgba(150, 170, 220, 0.04),
    0 4px 24px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.35),
    0 0 0 1px rgba(192, 200, 218, 0.06);
}

/* top-edge shimmer — light catching the glass surface */
.quote-card::before {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; left: 0; right: 0;
  height: 1px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    transparent              0%,
    rgba(220, 230, 255, 0.28) 20%,
    rgba(235, 242, 255, 0.45) 50%,
    rgba(220, 230, 255, 0.28) 80%,
    transparent              100%
  );
  pointer-events: none;
}

/* inner glow along left bezel */
.quote-card::after {
  content: '';
  position: absolute;
  top: 0; left: 0; bottom: 0;
  width: 40px;
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    rgba(192, 200, 218, 0.08) 0%,
    transparent              100%
  );
  pointer-events: none;
}

.quote-mark {
  position: absolute;
  top: 12px;
  left: 14px;
  font-size: 52px;
  line-height: 1;
  color: var(--accent);
  opacity: 0.72;
  font-family: Georgia, 'Times New Roman', serif;
  user-select: none;
}

.quote-text {
  margin: 0 0 20px;
  font-size: 15.5px;
  line-height: 1.75;
  color: var(--text-primary);
  font-style: italic;
  font-family: var(--font-content);
  letter-spacing: 0.01em;
}

.quote-attribution {
  display: flex;
  flex-direction: column;
  gap: 3px;
  padding-top: 14px;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(192, 200, 218, 0.14);
}

.quote-speaker {
  font-size: 13px;
  font-weight: 700;
  letter-spacing: 0.06em;
  color: rgba(210, 218, 235, 0.90);
  font-family: var(--font);
}

.quote-source {
  font-size: 11.5px;
  color: var(--text-muted);
  font-family: var(--font);
  letter-spacing: 0.02em;
}

/* light mode */
html.light .quote-card {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 252, 0.55);
  border-color: rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.30);
  border-left-color: rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.65);
  box-shadow:
    inset 0 1px 0 rgba(255, 230, 120, 0.18),
    0 4px 20px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.10),
    0 0 0 1px rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.08);
}
html.light .quote-card::before {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    transparent              0%,
    rgba(255, 220, 100, 0.30) 20%,
    rgba(255, 235, 140, 0.48) 50%,
    rgba(255, 220, 100, 0.30) 80%,
    transparent              100%
  );
}
html.light .quote-card::after {
  background: linear-gradient(90deg,
    rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.08) 0%,
    transparent              100%
  );
}
html.light .quote-mark {
  color: var(--accent);
  opacity: 0.80;
}
html.light .quote-text  { color: var(--text-primary); }
html.light .quote-speaker { color: #222222; }
html.light .quote-attribution { border-top-color: rgba(201, 162, 39, 0.20); }
</style>
